    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          An air purifier and a dehumidifier are not two versions of the same thing. A purifier catches what floats in the air: spores, pollen, dander, dust and smoke. A dehumidifier removes water vapor and does almost nothing to particles. If your problem is damp, a purifier will never fix it; if your problem is smoke or pollen, a dehumidifier will never fix it. Mold is the one case that usually needs both, and the order matters: dry the space first, then clean the air.
        </p>

        <div id="different-problems" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Two Machines, Two Different Problems
          </h2>

          <p>
            An air purifier is a fan pushing room air through a filter. In the units worth buying, that filter is true HEPA, certified to capture 99.97 percent of particles at 0.3 microns, often with a layer of activated carbon behind or around it for odors and gases. Air goes in dirty and comes out with fewer particles in it. The temperature does not change, the humidity does not change, and nothing in the room gets drier. A purifier is a particle machine and nothing else.
          </p>

          <p>
            A dehumidifier is a small refrigeration system. A fan draws air across a cold coil, water vapor condenses on the coil the way it does on a cold glass in summer, and the water drips into a bucket or down a drain hose. The air then passes over a warm coil and leaves the machine drier and a few degrees warmer than it went in. There is a filter on the intake, but it is a coarse mesh screen that exists to keep lint off the coils. It is not rated to catch anything fine, and no manufacturer publishes a clean air delivery rate for a dehumidifier because it does not have one.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the question to ask is not which machine is better, but what is actually wrong with the air. Things that float are a purifier job: mold spores already airborne, pollen, pet dander, fine dust, wildfire smoke, cooking smoke. Water is a dehumidifier job: condensation on windows or pipes, a damp basement, a musty smell that comes back every summer, humidity readings that sit above 60 percent. The two lists barely overlap, and the overlap is where most of the confusion comes from.
          </p>

          <p>
            The overlap is biological. Mold and dust mites are both particles you breathe and organisms that need moisture to live. A purifier deals with the particles they put into the air; a dehumidifier deals with the conditions that let them grow. That is why the honest answer for mold and for dust mite allergy is often both machines, and why the answer for nearly everything else is one or the other, not both.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE ONE-LINE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Particles: purifier. Water vapor: dehumidifier.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Before buying either, put a ten-dollar hygrometer in the room for three days. If it reads over 60 percent relative humidity, you have a moisture problem first. If it reads 30 to 50 percent and you still have symptoms or a smell, the problem is in the air, not the air's water content.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="by-problem" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Which One for Which Problem
          </h2>

          <p>
            The table below is the short version. Most people arrive here with one of six complaints, and for five of them the answer is a single machine. The sixth, mold, gets its own section because the order you do things in decides whether the money is well spent.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Problem</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Right machine</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Why</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Visible mold or mold returning</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Dehumidifier first, then purifier</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Growth needs moisture; airborne spores need filtering</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Musty basement smell</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Dehumidifier</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">The smell is produced by growth that thrives in damp air</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Pollen and pet dander allergy</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Air purifier</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Particles; humidity has little effect on them</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Dust mite allergy</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Both, dehumidifier often first</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mites need humidity above about 50 percent to thrive</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Household dust</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Air purifier, plus cleaning</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Dust is particles; a dehumidifier's mesh screen will not catch it</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Smoke, wildfire or cooking</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Air purifier with carbon</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fine particles and gases; no moisture involved</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Condensation, damp walls, sticky air</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Dehumidifier</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Too much water vapor; a purifier cannot remove it</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            <strong>Musty basement.</strong> This is the most common case where people buy a purifier and are disappointed. The musty smell comes from microbial volatile compounds released by mold and mildew growing on damp surfaces, usually because the basement sits above 60 percent humidity for weeks at a time. A purifier with a heavy carbon bed can mask the smell while it runs, but the source keeps producing it. A dehumidifier holding the space near 50 percent stops the growth that makes it. Our guide on whether <a href="/will-a-dehumidifier-get-rid-of-musty-basement-smell/">a dehumidifier will get rid of musty basement smell</a> explains why it takes several weeks rather than several days, and <a href="/do-i-need-a-dehumidifier-in-my-basement/">the three-day hygrometer test</a> tells you whether yours needs one at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Allergies.</strong> Pollen and pet dander are particles, they are airborne for long stretches, and a correctly sized HEPA purifier in the bedroom is the single most useful machine for them. Humidity does very little to either. Dust mites are the exception: they absorb water from the air rather than drinking it, and they struggle when relative humidity stays below about 50 percent. If your allergist has pinned it on mites and your home runs humid, the dehumidifier attacks the population and the purifier catches some of the allergen that gets stirred up, though mite allergen is heavy and settles quickly, so bedding and cleaning matter more than either machine. For the particle side, see <a href="/best-air-purifier-for-allergies/">our air purifiers for allergies</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Dust and smoke.</strong> These are purifier problems with no moisture component at all. Wildfire smoke and cooking smoke are mostly fine particles in the PM2.5 range, which HEPA handles well, plus gases and odors, which only activated carbon touches. A dehumidifier will do nothing useful here, and in a smoky room it will simply pull smoky air across its coils. If smoke is the reason you are shopping, go straight to <a href="/best-air-purifier-for-smoke/">our air purifiers for smoke</a> and skip the rest of this debate.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="mold" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Mold: Dehumidifier First, Purifier Second
          </h2>

          <p>
            Mold needs three things: a food source, which in a house is almost everything from drywall paper to dust, a suitable temperature, which a house always provides, and moisture. The only one you can realistically take away is moisture. Mold growth on household surfaces generally becomes a risk when relative humidity sits above about 60 percent for extended periods, and the EPA recommends keeping indoor humidity below 60 percent, ideally between 30 and 50 percent. A dehumidifier is the tool that holds a room in that range. A purifier does not move the humidity reading by a single point.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is the core of the dehumidifier vs air purifier for mold question. A HEPA purifier is very good at catching mold spores, which are typically 2 to 10 microns and therefore larger and easier to trap than the 0.3-micron particles HEPA is certified against. But spores in the air are the symptom. Growth on a damp surface is the source, and that colony will keep releasing spores for as long as it stays wet. Running a purifier in a 70 percent basement is filtering the output of a factory you have left switched on. We go through the evidence in detail in <a href="/do-air-purifiers-help-with-mold/">do air purifiers help with mold</a>, and the short answer is: with airborne spores, yes; with the mold itself, no.
          </p>

          <p>
            So if you can only buy one machine and you have a damp space with mold in it, buy the dehumidifier. Our <a href="/best-air-purifier-for-mold/">air purifier for mold guide</a> says the same thing in its own FAQ, which is worth noting because it is the page that would benefit from telling you otherwise. A dehumidifier stops new growth and, over a season, lets the existing growth dry out and go dormant. A purifier alone leaves the growth exactly where it is.
          </p>

          <p>
            The purifier earns its place in three situations. First, during and after cleanup: scrubbing or cutting out moldy material throws a large burst of spores and fragments into the air, and a purifier running in that room for the following days pulls them out before they settle elsewhere. Second, when someone in the house has asthma, a mold allergy or a weakened immune system, and the airborne load matters even while the moisture problem is being fixed. Third, in rooms where the humidity is already under control but spores drift in from somewhere else, such as a bedroom above a basement that is still being dealt with.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Numbers That Matter for Mold</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">30-50%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">EPA ideal indoor humidity range</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">60%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Upper limit; growth risk rises above it</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">2-10 µm</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical mold spore size</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">24-48 hrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">EPA window to dry wet materials</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Neither machine replaces fixing the water. If the moisture comes from a leak, a missing gutter extension, a wet crawlspace or a dryer venting indoors, both machines are working against a hose that is still running. The EPA's guidance is to dry wet materials within 24 to 48 hours to prevent growth, and to clean up small areas yourself while calling in professionals for larger ones, roughly anything over 10 square feet. Our sibling guide on <a href="/how-to-get-rid-of-mold-in-basement/">how to get rid of mold in a basement</a> covers the cleanup steps and where the line sits between a weekend job and a remediation contractor.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="both" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. When You Need Both, and in What Order
          </h2>

          <p>
            Running both machines makes sense in a narrow set of cases: active or recent mold, a damp space that someone sleeps or works in, a dust mite allergy in a humid home, and a finished basement that is both damp and lived in. In those cases the order is not a detail. Doing it backwards means paying to filter spores from a room that is still growing more of them.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Measure, then find the water</h3>

          <p>
            Put a hygrometer in the room for three days, ideally through a humid stretch. If it reads under 50 percent and there is still mold, the moisture is local: a leak, condensation on a cold pipe or wall, a wet spot from the outside. Fix that and a dehumidifier may not be needed. If it reads 60 percent or higher across the board, the space itself is damp and needs a dehumidifier sized for it. Look at grading, gutters and downspouts while you are at it, because outside water is the most common reason a basement reads high.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Dehumidify to 45-50 percent and hold it</h3>

          <p>
            Set the dehumidifier to 45 or 50 percent and leave it there, draining continuously if you possibly can. A bucket that fills overnight and stops the machine until someone empties it is how a basement goes back above 60 percent while you are on holiday. Size the unit for the dampness, not just the floor area; our guide to <a href="/what-size-dehumidifier-for-basement/">what size dehumidifier a basement needs</a> has the correction factors, and <a href="/what-should-a-basement-dehumidifier-be-set-at/">what a basement dehumidifier should be set at</a> covers the exceptions to the 50 percent rule. If one portable unit cannot keep up across a large or divided basement, our <a href="/whole-house-vs-portable-dehumidifier/">whole-house vs portable dehumidifier</a> comparison explains when a ducted system is the cheaper long-term answer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Clean visible growth, with the purifier running</h3>

          <p>
            Once the space is drying, clean or remove the visible growth. This is the moment to start the purifier, on a high fan setting, in the same room, and to keep it running for several days afterward. Disturbing mold is the single biggest spike in airborne spores you will see, and catching that spike is where a purifier does its most valuable work. Close the room off from the rest of the house while you work if you can.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Keep the purifier for people, the dehumidifier for the space</h3>

          <p>
            After cleanup, the dehumidifier stays on through the humid months indefinitely; it is maintenance, not treatment. The purifier can move to where people actually breathe, usually a bedroom, unless the basement itself is a living space. If they share a room, keep them a few feet apart so the dehumidifier's warm exhaust is not blowing straight into the purifier's intake, and so neither is pressed against a wall where it starves for air.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="running-costs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Running Costs: Watts and Dollars
          </h2>

          <p>
            The power gap between the two is large, and it is the reason a dehumidifier needs a humidistat and a purifier does not much care. A compressor dehumidifier draws several hundred watts while the compressor runs: roughly 300 to 450 watts for smaller units and 480 to 560 watts for a 50-pint basement unit, according to the figures in our guide to <a href="/how-much-electricity-does-a-dehumidifier-use/">how much electricity a dehumidifier uses</a>. A HEPA purifier draws a fraction of that. Manufacturer and Energy Star data put most mid-size and large units somewhere between about 5 watts on the lowest speed and 50 to 70 watts flat out.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Machine</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical draw</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Daily use</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Approx. daily cost at 17 cents/kWh</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">50-pint dehumidifier, July basement</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">480-560 W while running</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 7.5 kWh at 60 percent duty cycle</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1.25</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">30-pint dehumidifier, smaller space</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">300-450 W while running</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 5 kWh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $0.90</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Mid-size HEPA purifier, low-medium speed 24/7</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10-20 W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0.25-0.5 kWh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 4-8 cents</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Large HEPA purifier, high speed 24/7</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">50-65 W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1.2-1.6 kWh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 20-27 cents</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Those are ranges because both machines vary with how hard they work. A dehumidifier cycles off when it reaches its set point, so the same machine costs a dollar a day in a July basement and next to nothing in a dry January. A purifier runs continuously but at whatever fan speed you choose. The Levoit Core 600S, for example, lists around 7 to 11 watts on its two lower speeds and around 48 watts on its highest, and the Coway Airmega 400's Energy Star listing shows 64 watts in its smart mode. Run on a medium setting all year, a purifier like that costs in the region of $20 to $40 at 17 cents per kilowatt-hour.
          </p>

          <p>
            A dehumidifier, by contrast, costs more like $100 to $150 over a humid season in a damp basement. That is not a reason to skip one if you need it. It is a reason to get the size right, drain it continuously, and set it to 50 percent rather than 35, because every point below what you actually need is paid for in compressor hours. Integrated Energy Factor, the litres of water removed per kilowatt-hour, is the figure to compare between dehumidifiers; it is far more useful than the wattage on the box.
          </p>

          <p>
            For purifiers, the electricity is usually the smaller part of the running cost. Replacement filters are the bigger line item: tens of dollars a year for a budget unit, and well over a hundred for a premium one such as the IQAir HealthPro Plus, whose filters our pillar puts at $150 to $220 a year. Over five years, a mid-range purifier's filters can cost more than the machine did. Check filter price and availability before you buy, not after.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="noise-maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Noise and Maintenance: Filters vs Buckets
          </h2>

          <p>
            Noise is where the two feel most different to live with. A good purifier on its lowest setting is close to silent; the Coway Airmega 400 and Blueair Blue Pure 211+ both measured under 25 decibels on low in the testing described in our <a href="/best-air-purifier-for-mold/">air purifier for mold roundup</a>. On high, most purifiers become a steady fan hiss in the 50s. The sound is constant, which most people stop noticing after a night or two.
          </p>

          <p>
            A dehumidifier is a compressor appliance, and spec sheets for 50-pint portables generally list figures somewhere in the 40s to mid-50s dBA depending on fan setting. What bothers people more than the level is the cycling: the compressor kicks on with a thunk, runs, and stops, and the change is more noticeable than a steady hum. That is fine in an unfinished basement and irritating in a bedroom. If you need dehumidification in a room where people sleep, it is usually better to fix the source room below and let the rest of the house dry with it, or to choose a unit with a quiet fan mode.
          </p>

          <p>
            Maintenance on a purifier is mostly filters. Vacuum or rinse the pre-filter every few weeks, replace the HEPA filter roughly every 6 to 12 months depending on the model and how hard it works, and expect the carbon stage to saturate faster than the label suggests in a musty or smoky room. Our mold roundup found the GermGuardian's carbon filter needed changing every 3 to 4 months in a mold-affected space rather than the 6 to 8 on the label. A clogged filter does not make the air dirtier, but it does cut airflow, so the machine quietly cleans less air per hour.
          </p>

          <p>
            Maintenance on a dehumidifier is mostly water. Without a drain hose, a 50-pint unit in a genuinely damp basement can fill its bucket more than once a day, and it shuts off when the bucket is full, so continuous drainage to a floor drain or a condensate pump is close to essential in any space that needs one. Wash the intake mesh every few weeks, as most manuals recommend, and rinse the bucket regularly, because standing water in a dark plastic tub grows its own slime. A dehumidifier left to grow mildew in its own bucket is not a rare sight. Check that the drain hose has not kinked or lost its fall at the start of each season.
          </p>

          <p>
            Over a year, then, a purifier asks for a few minutes a month and a filter order once or twice a year. A dehumidifier asks for either a daily bucket trip or a one-time afternoon setting up a drain, plus a monthly clean. Neither is hard. The one that goes wrong is the one nobody set up properly, which for dehumidifiers is almost always the drain.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="decide" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How to Decide in Ten Minutes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with the hygrometer reading, because it settles most cases on its own. Over 60 percent for days at a time, or condensation you can see, means you need a dehumidifier whatever else is going on. Under 50 percent with symptoms, dust or a smell still present means the problem is particles or a local source, and a purifier, or finding that source, is the answer. In between, look at the season: a basement at 55 percent in October is often at 70 in July.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, ask what you are trying to protect: the building or the people. Dehumidifiers protect buildings. They stop mold on joists, rust on tools, warped stored furniture and the musty smell that soaks into boxes. Purifiers protect lungs. They reduce what someone with asthma or allergies actually inhales, and they matter most in the rooms where people spend the night. A damp unfinished basement nobody uses needs the first. A dry bedroom with an allergic child needs the second.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you land on a dehumidifier, our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">best basement dehumidifiers</a> roundup ranks the units worth buying, including the Frigidaire FFAD5033W1 and, for basements without a floor drain, the GE APER50LZ with a built-in pump. If you land on a purifier, size it before you shop: manufacturers rate coverage at two air changes per hour, and allergy or mold relief needs four to five, which our <a href="/what-size-air-purifier-do-i-need/">what size air purifier do I need</a> guide turns into a CADR number for your room.
          </p>

          <p>
            For mold specifically, the pillar picks are the place to start: the Coway Airmega 400 for a whole basement level, the Austin Air HealthMate Plus where the musty odor is the main complaint, and the Winix 5500-2 if you only need to cover a single bedroom or bathroom. All three are listed with sizing and filter costs in our <a href="/best-air-purifier-for-mold/">best air purifier for mold</a> guide. Pair any of them with a dehumidifier in the space that is actually damp, not in the bedroom where the purifier lives.
          </p>

          <p>
            A word on combination units. A handful of machines sell themselves as a dehumidifier and air purifier in one. They can be convenient in a small room with a mild problem, but the purifier side is usually a modest filter on a fan sized for dehumidifying, not for moving the volume of air a dedicated purifier does, and the manufacturers rarely publish a CADR to let you check. If you have a real mold or allergy problem, two specialised machines will outperform one compromise, and each can be replaced on its own when it wears out.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a purifier for a damp basement</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A purifier does not change humidity at all. In a basement sitting at 65 or 70 percent, it filters spores from a room that keeps growing more, and the musty smell returns the day you switch it off. Measure humidity first; if it is over 60 percent, the dehumidifier comes first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting a dehumidifier to clean the air</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The filter on a dehumidifier is a coarse mesh that protects the coils. It is not HEPA and has no clean air delivery rate, so it does nothing meaningful for pollen, dander, fine dust or smoke. If your symptoms are allergy-driven and the room is not damp, it is the wrong machine.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Setting the dehumidifier far too low</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Dropping the set point to 30 or 35 percent does not prevent mold any better than 45 to 50 percent does. It runs the compressor for hours longer, raises the electric bill noticeably, and in a living space can leave the air uncomfortably dry. Hold 45 to 50 percent and leave it there.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running the purifier only after cleaning mold</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The biggest spike in airborne spores happens while you scrub or cut out moldy material, not afterward. Start the purifier on high in that room before you begin, keep doors closed, and leave it running for several days after the visible growth is gone.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting ozone or ionizer claims for mold</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ozone generators sold as air cleaners are not an effective way to deal with mold and can irritate lungs at the levels needed to do anything. Ionizer modes on purifiers add little. Choose true HEPA for spores and real activated carbon for odor, and switch ionizer features off if you are sensitive.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is an air purifier or a dehumidifier better for mold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A dehumidifier, if you can only have one and the space is damp. Mold growth depends on moisture, and keeping humidity between 30 and 50 percent stops new growth. An air purifier removes spores already in the air but does nothing to the colony producing them. Ideally run both, drying the space first and adding the purifier during cleanup.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need an air purifier or a dehumidifier for allergies?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For pollen and pet dander, an air purifier, because those allergens are airborne particles and humidity has little effect on them. For dust mites, a dehumidifier helps too, since mites struggle when humidity stays below about 50 percent. If your home reads over 60 percent humidity and you are allergic to mites, both machines are justified.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a dehumidifier work as an air purifier?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. A dehumidifier's intake filter is a coarse mesh that keeps lint off the coils. It is not rated to capture fine particles, and no dehumidifier publishes a clean air delivery rate. It will catch some larger dust and hair, but it will not meaningfully reduce pollen, smoke, dander or mold spores in the air.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I run an air purifier and a dehumidifier in the same room?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and in a damp room with mold it is often the right setup. Keep them a few feet apart so the dehumidifier's warm exhaust does not blow straight into the purifier's intake, give both clearance from walls, and drain the dehumidifier continuously so it does not stop overnight when the bucket fills.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which uses more electricity, an air purifier or a dehumidifier?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A dehumidifier, by a wide margin. A 50-pint unit draws roughly 480 to 560 watts while its compressor runs and can cost around $1.25 a day in a humid basement. A HEPA purifier draws roughly 5 to 65 watts depending on fan speed, so running one all day typically costs between a few cents and about 25 cents.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will an air purifier get rid of a musty basement smell?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only while it runs, and only partly. A heavy carbon filter adsorbs the compounds that cause the smell, but the mold or mildew producing them keeps going as long as the basement stays damp. A dehumidifier holding the space near 50 percent removes the cause, and the smell usually fades over several weeks.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What humidity should I keep my home at to prevent mold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The EPA recommends keeping indoor relative humidity below 60 percent, ideally between 30 and 50 percent. Most people set a basement dehumidifier to 45 or 50 percent, which keeps mold and dust mites in check without overworking the compressor. Below 30 percent the air starts to feel uncomfortably dry for most people.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

